WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.747
Machining is making a part by cutting material away, in small curls called chips.

2
00:00:07.297 --> 00:00:11.885
You start too big, and cut until the shape on the drawing is left.

3
00:00:12.436 --> 00:00:17.871
It is precise and flexible, but every gram that comes off costs machine time.

4
00:00:18.771 --> 00:00:20.582
Follow this gearbox shaft.

5
00:00:21.132 --> 00:00:25.361
It starts as a steel bar, 55 millimetres thick.

6
00:00:25.911 --> 00:00:30.418
The routing says which machines it visits, and in which order.

7
00:00:31.318 --> 00:00:32.734
Step one: sawing.

8
00:00:33.284 --> 00:00:35.919
The blade comes down through the clamped bar.

9
00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:38.769
In 1 minute it has the right length.

10
00:00:39.969 --> 00:00:44.593
Step two: the CNC lathe, a lathe controlled by a computer.

11
00:00:45.142 --> 00:00:48.721
The chuck grips the shaft, and spins it fast.

12
00:00:49.621 --> 00:00:54.593
That is turning: the part spins, and a fixed cutting tool moves along it.

13
00:00:55.493 --> 00:01:00.233
That is how you make everything round: shafts, bushes, flanges.

14
00:01:00.783 --> 00:01:04.069
Here the tool turns the shaft to 50 millimetres,

15
00:01:04.268 --> 00:01:06.776
with shoulders where the diameter steps.

16
00:01:07.326 --> 00:01:09.091
That takes 6 minutes.

17
00:01:09.991 --> 00:01:16.461
On the 35 millimetre bearing seat, 0.2 millimetres is left on on purpose.

18
00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.309
That only comes off in grinding.

19
00:01:20.509 --> 00:01:22.994
No machine makes a size exactly.

20
00:01:23.544 --> 00:01:27.642
So the drawing gives a tolerance: how far a size may deviate.

21
00:01:28.542 --> 00:01:33.792
Turning holds about 0.02 millimetres, plus or minus.

22
00:01:34.342 --> 00:01:41.122
So this 50 millimetre shoulder may come out between 49.98 and 50.02.

23
00:01:42.322 --> 00:01:46.353
Step three: the machining centre, for what is not round.

24
00:01:46.903 --> 00:01:49.004
Here it is the other way round.

25
00:01:49.555 --> 00:01:51.958
In milling the tool spins: the cutter.

26
00:01:52.858 --> 00:01:56.842
The part is clamped on the table, and moves under the cutter.

27
00:01:57.393 --> 00:02:01.471
That is how you make flat faces, slots and recesses.

28
00:02:02.371 --> 00:02:07.703
Here it mills a keyway: a slot 10 millimetres wide, in 3 minutes.

29
00:02:08.603 --> 00:02:11.949
A key, a small steel block, sits in it.

30
00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:14.902
That way a gear turns with the shaft.

31
00:02:16.102 --> 00:02:20.296
Drilling is mostly done on the same machine, with a spinning drill.

32
00:02:20.846 --> 00:02:26.024
A drilled hole comes out a little big: a few hundredths to a tenth of a millimetre.

33
00:02:26.924 --> 00:02:30.862
If the hole must be exact, you drill slightly small.

34
00:02:31.412 --> 00:02:36.695
A reamer then takes off the last 0.1 to 0.3 millimetres.

35
00:02:37.895 --> 00:02:41.506
Step four: 3 days at an outside heat treater.

36
00:02:42.056 --> 00:02:45.890
It heats the steel, and then cools it very fast.

37
00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:51.379
Hard steel wears much less, and that is what you want under a bearing.

38
00:02:51.929 --> 00:02:54.460
But hardening warps the shaft slightly.

39
00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:57.425
So the final size comes after.

40
00:02:58.625 --> 00:03:01.191
Step five: the cylindrical grinder.

41
00:03:01.741 --> 00:03:06.550
The shaft turns between two centres, against a fast-spinning wheel.

42
00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:12.886
In grinding hard grains take off tiny amounts, like fine sandpaper.

43
00:03:13.436 --> 00:03:18.675
That works on hardened steel too, where ordinary tools soon wear out.

44
00:03:19.575 --> 00:03:24.814
That is how the last 0.2 millimetres comes off, in 4 minutes.

45
00:03:25.364 --> 00:03:31.808
Grinding holds 0.005 millimetres or better: four times tighter than turning.

46
00:03:32.708 --> 00:03:38.248
That is needed, because the bearing seat allows only 16 micrometres.

47
00:03:38.799 --> 00:03:43.584
But grinding is slow, so you grind only where the drawing demands it.

48
00:03:44.784 --> 00:03:46.909
Look again at the finished shaft.

49
00:03:47.459 --> 00:03:49.793
The round parts come from the lathe,

50
00:03:50.693 --> 00:03:54.457
the keyway from the cutter, the hole from drill and reamer,

51
00:03:55.357 --> 00:03:57.691
and the bearing seat from the grinder.

52
00:03:58.591 --> 00:04:01.447
All the cutting together takes 14 minutes.

53
00:04:01.997 --> 00:04:06.188
But from start to finish it takes about 6 days: mostly hardening,

54
00:04:06.388 --> 00:04:07.932
transport and waiting.

55
00:04:08.482 --> 00:04:11.501
The routing shows where the time really goes.
